00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.830
in the first ever moving image

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.780
created by Eadweard Muybridge.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.610
Now, while the moving image is real,

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.020
Peele made up the anonymous

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.030
black jockey's identity for the film.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.030
So on camera spectacle
clearly runs in the family.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.520
After their father mysteriously dies,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.820
OJ notices something strange in the sky.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.010
You're probably not
stunned that what he sees

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.900
looks like an alien spacecraft.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.330
But instead of wisely
choosing to get the out

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.520
and never look back like I would,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.730
they decide to try and
capture the site on camera

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.910
so they can get there

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.500
because why worry about
potentially being zapped

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.980
or probed to death by aliens
so long as you go viral.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.930
And this feels like the first indication

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.460
that Peele wants us to reflect

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.040
on our own societal
fascination with spectacle

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.110
whether we're creating it, filming it

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.720
or simply consuming the
potentially horrible

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.520
and shocking sites from a tiny screen

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.990
as we sit on the toilet,

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.210
none of us seem to be immune.

