00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.470
We're gonna explore this idea

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.840
with the help of a French philosopher.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.490
I bet you didn't see that one coming,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.190
but seriously we can't
help it that the French

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.080
are good at using philosophy to analyze

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.970
the decline of human culture.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.710
And one of these folks Guy Debord

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:39.090
wrote a book in 1967 called,"
the Society of the Spectacle"

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.207
which opens with the line,

00:03:40.207 --> 00:03:43.680
"in societies or modern
conditions of production prevail,

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.270
all of life presents itself

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.970
as an immense accumulation of spectacles."

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:51.450
For Debord, the modern
spectacle has turned a society

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.180
primarily based on
relations between humans

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.330
into one that prioritizes
relations between commodities

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.790
i.e, economic relations matter

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.920
more than human beings.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.930
And we see a bit of this in an early scene

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.060
where OJ is treated like,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.150
by some commercial director
and his real life horse

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.910
is ultimately replaced by a CGI stand-in.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.680
According to de bore's definition,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.100
spectacle is perceived in
three ways all at once,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.220
as all of society, as part of society

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.380
and as instrument of unification.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.620
For him, as spectacle underlies
all social perspectives

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.510
and all consciousness,

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.880
as he explains, "due to the very fact

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.290
that this sector is separate,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.990
it is the common ground
of the deceased gaze

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.580
and of false consciousness

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.650
and the unification it achieves is nothing

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.940
but in all official language
of generalized separation"

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.660
I.e by keeping us all focused
on various spectacles,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.700
society turns us into zombies

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.520
with deceased gazes, isolated from others

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.470
and from our own humanity.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.600
Basically living in the spectacle,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.460
our constant impulse to talk about

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:59.430
if we've seen the new thing
or heard about what happened

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.050
or read the crazy tweets

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.210
or saw that weird guy's TikTok

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.640
or the politicians leaked nudes

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.670
becomes what society is for most of us.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.890
And in a world where this
is what is most real,

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.410
why wouldn't a brother and
sister in need of cash,

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.930
see capturing an alien ship on camera

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.300
as a way to really make
their lives matter?

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.940
When Debord talks about
the unification achieved

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.530
by the spectacle being

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.860
an official language of
generalized separation,

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.600
he's getting at the way in which

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.510
our gaze being caught in the spectacle,

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.130
alienates us from each other.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.830
When our heads are all
looking down at our phones,

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.570
we're not relating to anyone else.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.270
And we're definitely not
considering the humanity

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.530
of those on our screens

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.770
and ultimately that dehumanizes all of us.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.710
And in "Nope," when the ex child star Jupe

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.630
post the show at his Western theme park,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.390
we see everyone gazing up at the spectacle

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.160
with this deceased gaze.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.260
The message seemingly being,

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.110
show me something shocking
or else I'm not interested.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.780
And of course, here it's being
done for the sake of profit.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.570
For Debord, the alienation
of the spectator

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.090
to the profit of the contemplated object

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.100
is expressed in the following way,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.460
the more he contemplates,
the less he lives,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.800
the more he accepts recognizing himself

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.630
and the dominant images of need,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.390
the less he understands his own existence

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.890
and his own desires.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.890
I.e, the more we get
caught up in the images

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.810
and spectacles that are sold to us,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.020
the more we lose ourselves in the process.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.910
And in "Nope," we see what happens

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:33.510
when individuals get so
caught up in being spectators

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.780
that they risk losing
themselves in the spectacle.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.240
Now, while we might not
be risking our lives

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.400
